Fix conversions between pointer types so that the result behaves like a pointer of the destination type, the way it does in Go. This covers explicit conversions such as `P(p)` or `(*MyInt)(p)`, and the implicit conversions that happen wherever a `*T` value is used where an assignable named pointer type such as `type P *T` is expected, or the other way around.

A converted pointer has the destination type, as seen by `%T`, reflection, type assertions and type switches. It is nil exactly when the source is nil, and it refers to the same variable, so writes through either pointer are visible through the other. Converting a pointer to another type and back gives a pointer equal to the original, and converting the same pointer to the same type twice gives equal pointers, so converted pointers also work as map keys. This applies to pointers to basic types, including element types that differ only by name such as `*int` and `*MyInt`, to pointers to pointers and to pointers to arrays, including pointers to slice elements and struct fields.

`unsafe.Pointer` follows the same rule for nil. Converting the zero `unsafe.Pointer` to any pointer type gives that type's nil pointer, and converting a nil pointer of any type to `unsafe.Pointer` gives a value equal to nil. Pointers of any types that refer to the same variable give equal `unsafe.Pointer` values, which can be compared and used as map keys, and a non-nil pointer that goes through `unsafe.Pointer` and back to its own type keeps its identity. When it is converted to a different pointer type whose element type has the same memory layout, such as from `*int64` to `*MyInt64`, from `**int` to `**MyInt`, from `*[2]int64` to `*[2]MyInt64`, or between two struct types whose fields differ in that way, the result has the destination type and refers to the same variable. Values read through it have the destination types, down to the elements of arrays and the fields of structs it contains, while values written through it keep the types of the original variable.

Reflection follows the same rules as code. `reflect.Value.Convert` converts pointers like a conversion does, `reflect.NewAt` gives the same pointer as converting its `unsafe.Pointer` argument to a pointer to the given type, pointers stored by `reflect.Value.Set`, `SetMapIndex` or passed by `Call` take the type they are assigned to, and `reflect.Value.Pointer` and `UnsafePointer` give the same result for pointers of any types to the same variable.
